{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:14a2aac5a54d36efbf6a3a753d3dd2d4f7c57a79951c08b22631841717f7d57a","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.4},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1: 23 / 28 / 24, with conjunct A (MOVE 10-yr average >= 105) at 0.33 / 0.42 / 0.48. This is the clearest case in the panel of a correct observation made and then not priced. Panelist 3 states it exactly: \"MOVE is NORMALISED (basis-point) implied vol ... so its LEVEL scales with the level of yields. A persistent 4%+ nominal regime floors MOVE around 85-100 without any stress\" — and then concludes \"a 105 decade-average then requires 2022-23 conditions roughly a third of the time\" and sets A = 0.48. If the unstressed FLOOR is 85-100, a 105 average needs stress episodes lifting the mean ~10-20 points above floor, which is one or two 2022-style years plus ordinary 2024-style churn, not a third of the decade at 120-140. Panelist 1's reconstruction compounds the error by averaging historical MOVE levels across yield REGIMES without rescaling: his \"1988-1997 and 1990-1999 likely >=105\" windows sat on 6-8% nominal yields and his \"2010-2019 ~70\" sat on 1.5-2.5% — those are not comparable observations of the same quantity, and treating them as one reference class is the base-rate equivalent of adding nominal and real series. My CQ4 P50 of 2.2% real plus ~2.5% OECD inflation implies a US 10y nominal averaging ~4.5-5.0% through 2027-2036. In that regime the bp-vol floor alone is ~90-100. MARGINAL A = 0.60.\n\nConjunct B. Sub-step 1, at least one MSCI World >=25% peak-to-trough drawdown in 2027-2036: I accept Wave 1's 0.78-0.85 and use 0.80 — 2B AI-Q4 at 66% already posits an impairment or revenue-coverage failure, index concentration in AI-levered names is extreme, the $1.4T commitment stack rests on ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt, the IMF scenario is explicit at 20%, and USD denomination plus a price (not total-return) basis each make the 25% threshold easier.\nSub-step 2, conditional \"no-rally\" (G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominals fail to fall >=50bp, or rise >=25bp, inside the drawdown window). Wave 1: 0.39 / 0.55 / 0.40. Panelist 1's regime-sorting is the right frame and his own evidence supports a HIGHER conditional than he used: five of eight historical episodes failed, and he discounts to 0.39 by assigning only ~30% weight to the inflation-salient regime — a weight contradicted by his own CQ2 OECD-core marginal and flatly contradicted by my 0.84. Add two structural points Panelist 3 raised and under-priced: under the Section-locked PPP weights Japan is ~13% and Italy ~7% of the aggregate, Japan cannot contribute a 50bp rally from a ~2% nominal, and Italian yields typically RISE in risk-off as spreads widen — so a PPP-weighted G7 aggregate is mechanically less able to rally 50bp than the US alone. Plus the undefined \"drawdown window\": the equity trough coincides with the yield PEAK in inflation- or supply-driven selloffs, which favours YES. Conditional = 0.55 for at least one qualifying episode given ~1.5-2 expected drawdowns. MARGINAL B = 0.80 x 0.55 = 0.44.\nCORRELATION: strongly positive, rho ~ +0.6 — a high-bp-vol decade is definitionally the regime in which the equity-bond hedge fails; both legs are one bet on inflation salience and duration-supply plumbing. P(B|A) = 0.70.\nJOINT = 0.60 x 0.70 = 0.42, trimmed to 40 for residual conjunction humility and for the fact that ICE can reconstitute or discontinue the index. ANSWER 40, versus Wave 1's 23-28. This is my largest single break from the Wave-1 centre and it rests on one technical fact about the resolving series that a Wave-1 panelist wrote down and declined to use.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.486Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"NBS reports a calendar-year real GDP growth rate below 3.5% for any year, absent a declared pandemic or war shock, as reprinted in the IMF WEO.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the reported-series institutional floor, my single largest CQ3 driver. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1% to ~1.8% and P90 to ~2.8%; CQ6-A falls from 22 to ~13 and D rises from 7 to ~12; CQ4 P50 falls ~15bp on the lower-g channel. This is the cleanest single falsifier of my whole payload.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS data (OES median hourly by industry, or ECI by occupation group) show healthcare-support plus construction-and-extraction real median hourly wages cumulatively BELOW information plus finance plus professional-and-business-services, measured from 2025, for two consecutive annual vintages.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my claim that the required F+Q vs J+K+M divergence is a continuation of an already-running trend rather than a sign flip. CQ1 falls from 23 to ~10, and plank (a) stays at 5 while my attack on Wave 1's dilution contradiction loses its empirical leg.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Türkiye core CPI 12-month average below 5% AND Mexico, Poland and Chile core CPI all below 3%, sustained for four consecutive quarters.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the PPP-weighting composition floor in the OECD-38 core aggregate. CQ2 falls from 40 to ~30 (OECD conjunct 0.84 -> ~0.62); CQ6-C falls from 23 to ~16 and D rises from 7 to ~13; plank (c) rises from 20 to ~32.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE trailing five-year average below 90 while the US 10-year nominal Treasury yield averaged at or above 4.0% over the same five years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the bp-normalization argument that drove my CQ5 away from the Wave-1 centre — it would show the vol floor does NOT scale with the yield level. CQ5 falls from 40 to ~22, i.e. back onto the Wave-1 estimate, and the fiscal/term-premium transmission channel underpinning my CQ4 P90 of 3.4% weakens.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment rate prints at or above 7.0% for twelve consecutive months, or OECD-38 aggregate population growth turns negative on the OECD's own population controls.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Either observation breaks my CQ1 conjunct-1 denominator mechanism (0.62) and my +0.25%/yr OECD population term. CQ1 falls from 23 to ~8; CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1% to ~1.7% and P10 to ~0.9%; CQ6-D rises from 7 to ~18 and A falls from 22 to ~12, converging my CQ6 onto Panelist 1's.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 redteam seat, effort 'labor'."}}